<Profile title="Approach — Dave Bettner" page="fit" description="How Dave Bettner takes enterprise workflows from requirements and product decisions through implementation, testing, training, and client ownership.">
  <section class="page-intro wrap">
    <h1>Start with the workflow.<br/>Build the experience.</h1>
    <p class="lead">What is someone trying to do? What information do they need next? Where should automation help? I work from those questions through interface design and implementation, using AI coding tools throughout.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="paper">
    <div class="section wrap prose-grid">
      <p class="aside">Customer understanding shapes the product decisions. AI coding tools help me carry those decisions into working software.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <section id="building">
          <h2>Own the experience from direction through implementation.</h2>
          <p>For Homebase, Passal, and Leasekite, I own product direction, interface design, and implementation through AI coding tools. I use those tools to explore alternatives, build the flow, and work through changes.</p>
          <p>My main project, <a href="/work/homebase/">Homebase</a>, connects business records, project work, content, and agent assignments in a private operating workspace. Its design keeps owners, review steps, and delivery records attached to the work.</p>
          <p>In Passal, the workflow connects lecture sources to study choices and practice. In Leasekite, it brings vehicle comparison and lease requests together while keeping drafts available for review and negotiation decisions with the user.</p>
          <a href="/work/">See the products and their design choices</a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Understand the problem before proposing a solution.</h2>
          <p>I run requirements sessions with the people using the system. We work through workflows, data sources, access, exceptions, and what success would look like. The aim is a concrete problem to solve, not a list of features to configure.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="scope">
          <h2>Set scope around client ownership.</h2>
          <p>In a recent implementation, I reset expectations around who would do the work. Our role was to enable the client team and help them get comfortable doing it themselves, rather than do all of the work for them.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Turn recurring delivery work into reusable tools.</h2>
          <p>At LSL, I develop Python tools that generate reporting links, formulas, and validation checks. They are reused across financial-reporting and budgeting engagements. One internal AI-assisted cash-flow review workflow covered 11 source workpapers with 69 automated arithmetic checks, flagging discrepancies for human review before client changes. These workflows are separate from my synthetic public demos.</p>
          <a href="/ai-workflows/">Explore my AI workflows and agent stack</a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Carry the work through acceptance.</h2>
          <p>I stay involved through user acceptance testing, training, and go-live. Consultant coaching and repeatable delivery guidance help the team support the implementation. Recurring customer issues also become specific feedback for Product and Engineering.</p>
          <a href="/experience/connected-reporting/">See a connected-reporting implementation</a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Explain the decisions through a working example.</h2>
          <p>Customer demos, requirements workshops, training, and consultant coaching are part of my delivery work. I use concrete examples to connect what a system does with the decisions a team needs to make.</p>
          <p>My public workflow lesson lets a visitor check an automated update, reject stale or incorrect proposals, and recover when a response is lost. The lesson uses fictional inventory data and local checks, so the behavior is open to inspection.</p>
          <a href="/learn/verify-an-ai-update/">Try the interactive lesson</a>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Choose where automation belongs.</h2>
          <p>In Leasekite, I keep routine status updates tied to recorded quote and delivery state. The application prepares drafts for review; questions and negotiation still need human judgment. That distinction shapes the workflow I’m building.</p>
          <a href="/work/">See the technical projects</a>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Profile>
